{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "1-1_4", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "To help manage acid reflux, you should generally avoid or limit high-fat and fried foods, as they can relax the lower esophageal sphincter and delay stomach emptying. This includes items like fatty cuts of meat (pork, beef, lamb, bacon), full-fat dairy (whole milk, butter, sour cream, most cheeses), ice cream, potato chips, french fries, and creamy sauces.\n\nHighly acidic fruits and vegetables are also common triggers. These include tomatoes (and products like sauce, ketchup), citrus fruits (lemons, oranges, grapefruit, pineapple), pomegranates, onions (especially raw), and garlic. Some spices and herbs like hot chili peppers, black pepper, peppermint, and spearmint can also worsen symptoms.\n\nBeverages such as coffee, most caffeinated teas, carbonated drinks (sodas), and alcohol are often problematic. Chocolate is another common trigger due to its caffeine, fat, and cocoa content.\n\nBeyond specific foods, eating too much at once or eating too late in the evening before lying down can also trigger acid reflux. It's often helpful to identify your personal triggers by keeping a food diary.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0034-09-00524:2": 4.842056517867681, "clueweb22-en0013-66-13847:15": 4.76963010239341, "clueweb22-en0037-98-07152:4": 4.694106067463636, "clueweb22-en0004-59-18085:9": 4.6818925971444205, "clueweb22-en0018-87-10874:12": 4.62259843872539, "clueweb22-en0018-50-11252:14": 4.590145677211143, "clueweb22-en0013-66-13847:14": 4.5757751756631375, "clueweb22-en0044-72-05971:4": 4.5488275061864085, "clueweb22-en0008-00-02353:4": 4.451944112114778, "clueweb22-en0004-59-18085:10": 4.445349080722875, "clueweb22-en0006-64-08980:4": 4.436352683335482, "clueweb22-en0018-87-10874:11": 4.356970831198962, "clueweb22-en0020-31-05749:4": 4.30264258164347, "clueweb22-en0020-31-05749:5": 4.29231460298997, "clueweb22-en0015-73-0008