{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "1-1_5", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "To help manage your acid reflux, especially considering your habit of eating dinner late and recent weight gain, several lifestyle adjustments can make a significant difference.\n\nFirstly, regarding your late dinners, it's highly recommended to avoid eating within two to three hours of bedtime. This allows your stomach sufficient time to digest food before you lie down, which minimizes the risk of acid reflux. Staying upright after meals, or even taking a short walk, can also be beneficial.\n\nSecondly, managing your weight is crucial. Losing weight, particularly if you've gained recently, can significantly reduce the pressure on your stomach and lessen acid reflux symptoms. Regular exercise and a balanced diet are key components of weight management and overall lifestyle change.\n\nBeyond these, consider eating smaller, more frequent meals throughout the day instead of large ones, as overeating can trigger reflux. Wearing loose-fitting clothing, especially around your abdomen, can also help. Elevating the head of your bed by 4-6 inches (not just with pillows) can prevent acid from flowing back into your esophagus during sleep. Additionally, managing stress through activities like meditation or ensuring adequate sleep can indirectly help, as stress can worsen symptoms. Avoiding smoking and limiting alcohol intake are also important for overall digestive health.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0034-91-08129:2": 4.788256076345358, "clueweb22-en0037-74-14945:10": 4.6464253055013005, "clueweb22-en0008-04-03010:3": 4.5029675883183895, "clueweb22-en0005-19-04103:5": 4.34965175804059, "clueweb22-en0003-24-03853:5": 4.231944574431487, "clueweb22-en0016-83-09082:8": 4.165011477270977, "clueweb22-en0034-91-08129:1": 4.126732640393716, "clueweb22-en0038-68-03750:10": 4.076340238614122, "clueweb22-en0009-95-06055:10": 4.060312152774392,