{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "1-1_6", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Yes, you are correct, cider vinegar (specifically apple cider vinegar) is acidic, primarily due to acetic acid, giving it a low pH.\n\nThe rationale behind its recommendation for acid reflux, though counter-intuitive, stems from the theory that some cases of acid reflux are caused by *too little* stomach acid, rather than too much. Proponents suggest that consuming diluted apple cider vinegar can help increase stomach acidity to a healthy level, aiding digestion and signaling the lower esophageal sphincter to close properly, preventing acid from flowing back up.\n\nHowever, it's important to note that there is *no published scientific evidence* to widely support these claims. Many sources argue that adding more acid could worsen symptoms, especially for conditions like silent reflux (LPR), and potentially cause further irritation or damage to the esophagus. If you choose to try it, always dilute it significantly with water, as undiluted apple cider vinegar can irritate the esophagus and damage tooth enamel. It's best to consult a healthcare professional before using it as a remedy.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0007-10-18426:18": 4.8004776317153075, "clueweb22-en0027-96-01816:0": 4.513692952616206, "clueweb22-en0032-91-00840:17": 4.38578481306895, "clueweb22-en0042-89-04330:9": 4.220373118197612, "clueweb22-en0025-73-02622:9": 4.180491839295312, "clueweb22-en0002-87-15707:6": 4.012872386157278, "clueweb22-en0030-73-19245:5": 3.9453693944239063}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0007-10-18426:18": 4.8004776317153075, "clueweb22-en0038-17-12281:10": 4.613665067095155, "clueweb22-en0027-96-01816:0": 4.513692952616206, "clueweb22-en0038-17-12281:9": 4.507362328875642, "clueweb22-en0032-91-00840:17": 4.38578481306895, "clueweb22-en0030-73-19245:3": 4.321371803889395, "clueweb22-en0027-89-06860:0": 4.229074974