{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "1-1_9", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Proton Pump Inhibitors (PPIs) and H2 blockers both work to reduce stomach acid, but they do so differently and have varying effectiveness and uses.\n\n**Mechanism:** PPIs block the proton pump, which is the final step in stomach acid production. H2 blockers, on the other hand, block histamine receptors that stimulate acid secretion.\n\n**Effectiveness:** PPIs are generally considered more powerful and effective, especially for severe or chronic acid reflux and healing esophageal damage. H2 blockers are less potent but can be sufficient for milder cases.\n\n**Onset and Duration:** H2 blockers typically act faster, providing relief within 15-30 minutes, and their effects can last up to 12 hours. PPIs take longer to take effect but provide longer-lasting relief.\n\n**Usage:** H2 blockers are often recommended for mild, occasional, or short-term acid reflux. PPIs are preferred for frequent, severe, or chronic conditions like GERD. H2 blockers can also be less expensive.\n\n**Side Effects:** Both types of medications can have side effects with long-term use, including an increased risk of hip fracture and interference with nutrient absorption. PPIs have also been associated with an increased risk of chronic kidney disease and certain infections, while some H2 blockers have had carcinogen concerns.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0006-26-13955:3": 4.80978799732732, "clueweb22-en0012-22-15649:9": 4.804063448846819, "clueweb22-en0004-00-09704:7": 4.743218499748465, "clueweb22-en0016-53-08494:9": 4.65477585811669, "clueweb22-en0029-78-09970:5": 4.650869983865316, "clueweb22-en0016-53-08494:10": 4.605844140060908, "clueweb22-en0029-78-09970:6": 4.5687006052359616, "clueweb22-en0032-45-03257:16": 4.548751136245951, "clueweb22-en0024-51-00969:0": 4.509335381014203, "clueweb22-en0029-78-09970:4": 4.407671818408085, "clueweb22-en0012-44-09