{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "1-1_10", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) and H2 blockers both work to reduce stomach acid, but they do so through different mechanisms. PPIs work by inhibiting the hydrogen-potassium adenosine triphosphatase enzyme system, often referred to as the \"proton pump,\" located in the cells lining your stomach. This system is responsible for the final step in producing stomach acid, so by blocking it, PPIs significantly reduce acid secretion.\n\nH2 blockers, on the other hand, reduce acid production by blocking histamine receptors in the stomach lining. Histamine is a substance that stimulates these cells to produce gastric acid. By preventing histamine from attaching to these receptors, H2 blockers interfere with the signal for acid production, thereby decreasing the overall amount of stomach acid.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0034-18-07329:2": 4.904770354277789, "clueweb22-en0029-78-09970:5": 4.846277227950382, "clueweb22-en0018-78-05578:4": 4.524144549553331}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0034-18-07329:2": 4.904770354277789, "clueweb22-en0032-45-03257:16": 4.904344972091724, "clueweb22-en0004-00-09704:7": 4.888637459927115, "clueweb22-en0029-78-09970:5": 4.846277227950382, "clueweb22-en0016-53-08494:9": 4.7406053599740305, "clueweb22-en0029-78-09970:6": 4.713104864852722, "clueweb22-en0046-25-11674:5": 4.694060197022016, "clueweb22-en0016-53-08494:10": 4.681888461976864, "clueweb22-en0040-98-04604:3": 4.643601933519586, "clueweb22-en0018-00-12905:2": 4.618976588536852, "clueweb22-en0041-89-18148:7": 4.612252120558621, "clueweb22-en0041-97-07392:7": 4.603681691875985, "clueweb22-en0012-22-15649:4": 4.602936520060279, "clueweb22-en0043-49-02220:6": 4.6021277282683615, "clueweb22-en0016-83-09082:12": 4.591394652713295, "clueweb22-en0040-99-17751:20": 4.548321454113461, "clueweb22-en0029-78-09970:4": 4.5457498991591