{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "2-1_1", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "To make a good-tasting coffee that's also gentle on your stomach, consider a few approaches.\n\nFirstly, **choose low-acid coffee beans**. Darker roasts are generally less acidic than lighter roasts (Passages 2, 4, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11, 19). Arabica beans are also known to have less caffeine, which can trigger acid production (Passages 6, 14). Some brands specifically offer low-acid blends, such as LifeBoost Coffee, Puroast, Lucy Jo's, or Golden Ratio, which are designed to be easier on the stomach while maintaining flavor (Passages 1, 3, 9, 13, 17, 18). Beans grown at lower altitudes or shade-grown varieties can also be naturally less acidic (Passages 2, 5, 12, 14).\n\nSecondly, **change your brewing method to cold brew**. This method uses cold water to extract flavor, resulting in a coffee that is significantly less acidic (up to 70% fewer acids) and often described as smoother, fuller, and less bitter than hot-brewed coffee (Passages 4, 5, 6, 7, 10, 11, 12, 14, 15, 19, 20).\n\nLastly, you can **add acid-reducing ingredients** directly to your coffee or grounds. A small pinch of baking soda can neutralize acidity and even reduce the harsh flavor of some coffees without affecting the taste (Passages 1, 4, 11, 16, 17). Similarly, a pinch of salt can help smooth out the flavor and nullify acidic levels (Passages 4, 8, 10, 15, 20). Drinking coffee after a meal can also help prevent acid reflux (Passages 4, 10, 11).", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0045-23-13438:1": 3.992579629067011, "clueweb22-en0045-27-00467:13": 3.9648597166489945, "clueweb22-en0031-89-00714:0": 3.861831899987001, "clueweb22-en0017-76-18714:7": 3.669198166804443, "clueweb22-en0013-18-05190:7": 3.6267693237917387, "clueweb22-en0004-76-08271:12": 3.536637562820089, "clueweb22-en0045-27-00467:14": 3.5279595832275064, "clueweb22-en0016-94-10981:6": 3.4562806114304263,