{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "2-1_3", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "To make your coffee taste better and be gentler on your stomach, especially with acid reflux, consider these methods:\n\n**For Acidity and Taste:**\n*   **Additives to Grounds:** A pinch of **baking soda** (sodium bicarbonate) added to your coffee grounds before brewing can neutralize acidity and reduce harsh flavors (Passage 4, 12). Alternatively, well-rinsed, crushed **eggshells** placed in your coffee maker along with the grounds can reduce bitterness and acidity (Passage 2, 13).\n*   **Salt:** Adding a small pinch of **salt** (e.g., 1/16 to 1/8 teaspoon) to your coffee grounds before brewing can counteract bitterness, enhance natural flavors, and help ease acid reflux (Passage 7, 13, 16).\n*   **Coffee Bean Choice:** Opt for **darker roasts**, as they are generally less acidic (Passage 8). You can also look for low-acid varieties like Mexico or Sumatra coffees, or decaf processed with the Mountain water method (Passage 9).\n*   **Brew Method:** **Cold brewing** significantly reduces acidity (up to 70%) because it extracts fewer oils, making it a good choice for sensitive stomachs (Passage 8, 15).\n\n**For Overall Taste (using a coffee machine):**\n*   **Water Quality:** Use **filtered tap water** or **alkaline water**, as impurities in unfiltered water can affect flavor (Passage 1).\n*   **Machine Maintenance:** Regularly **clean your coffee machine** (e.g., by running vinegar through it) to prevent residue buildup that can cause a bitter or stale taste (Passage 7).\n*   **Brewing Time:** Avoid brewing your coffee for too long, as this can result in a burnt taste (Passage 1).", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0029-58-17697:7": 4.662751957165986, "clueweb22-en0015-13-06066:13": 4.399196836037831, "clueweb22-en0024-78-17645:5": 4.30875045970136, "clueweb22-en0039-92-06362:14": 4.16112751869469, "clueweb22-en0017-76-18714:7": 