{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "2-1_4", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "To prevent your coffee from tasting sour, which is often a sign of under-extraction, consider adjusting a few key factors:\n\nFirstly, **grind size** is crucial. If your coffee tastes sour, your grind might be too coarse, causing water to pass through too quickly without extracting enough flavor. Try making your grind size finer.\n\nSecondly, **brewing time** plays a significant role. Sourness can result from ending the extraction process too early. Extending the brew time allows more sugars and bitter compounds to balance the initial acidity. This is especially effective for immersion methods like French press.\n\nThirdly, ensure your **water temperature** is hot enough. Water that isn't hot enough can lead to under-extraction. Aim for a temperature between 205\u00b0 and 210\u00b0 Fahrenheit.\n\nFinally, check your **coffee-to-water ratio**. Using too little coffee grounds can also cause under-extraction and sourness. Increase the amount of coffee grounds relative to water to ensure proper extraction.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0011-03-12177:19": 4.9850605067456755, "clueweb22-en0035-48-11701:2": 4.688524092296466, "clueweb22-en0000-23-14012:4": 4.580655128217195, "clueweb22-en0043-72-15068:12": 4.573833666045824, "clueweb22-en0000-23-14012:8": 4.344368779471778, "clueweb22-en0000-23-14012:3": 4.306862369486976, "clueweb22-en0000-23-14012:6": 4.304937290968137, "clueweb22-en0011-03-12177:4": 4.298791657163453, "clueweb22-en0011-03-12177:8": 4.293278555305348, "clueweb22-en0035-48-11701:1": 4.260006998307398, "clueweb22-en0000-23-14012:9": 4.254686959124886}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0011-03-12177:19": 4.9850605067456755, "clueweb22-en0011-03-12177:18": 4.817076078699454, "clueweb22-en0035-48-11701:2": 4.688524092296466, "clueweb22-en0035-48-11701:3": 4.636282845679106, "clueweb22-en0011-03-12