{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "2-1_11", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Roasting coffee beans is a transformative process that profoundly impacts their physical and chemical properties, ultimately shaping the flavor, aroma, body, and acidity of your coffee.\n\nPhysically, roasting removes moisture from the beans, causing them to lose 12-20% of their weight, expand in volume, and become more porous, making them less dense and more soluble. The color changes from green to brown due to the production of melanoidins, and oils migrate to the surface.\n\nChemically, roasting triggers hundreds of reactions. Key among these are the Maillard reaction (between carbohydrates and amino acids) and caramelization of sugars, which create many of the desirable flavor and aroma compounds, including those responsible for sweetness. It also breaks down chlorogenic acid, influencing bitterness and astringency.\n\nThe degree of roast significantly influences the final taste profile. Lighter roasts retain more of the bean's original characteristics, including higher acidity and brighter floral or fruity notes. As roasting progresses to medium and dark levels, the inherent flavors of the bean are eclipsed by roasted notes, leading to a fuller body, reduced acidity, and often a more bittersweet or smoky taste. While roasting dramatically alters flavor and aroma, it has minimal impact on the caffeine content of the beans.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0034-86-14025:0": 4.3351758536319025, "clueweb22-en0040-04-13369:2": 4.3133009445026005, "clueweb22-en0022-32-07211:5": 4.247485408672292, "clueweb22-en0035-10-05441:8": 4.317553648088927, "clueweb22-en0005-59-07136:15": 4.163915659376688, "clueweb22-en0001-58-05338:11": 4.208415366227184, "clueweb22-en0040-04-13369:13": 4.160820001443945}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0041-01-05927:8": 4.9962251416972, "clueweb22-en0001-58-05338:1": 4.72791564915