{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "2-2_3", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "When discussing \"sour\" wines, it typically refers to wines with high acidity, which can be a natural characteristic or, if excessive, a fault. If you're interested in wines with intentional tartness or prominent acidity, several types fit this description. Traditionally made red wines from Europe, such as Rioja, many French reds, most Italian reds, and Chilean reds, are often described as dry and not sweet, with a noticeable sour (acidic) profile. Austrian Zweigelt is another option, known for its balance of sweet and sour notes. Lambrusco, a bubbly wine, can also offer a good mix of acidity and sweetness. Riesling wines, especially those from cooler climates, are known for their naturally high acid levels, though these are often balanced with sugar. Additionally, certain fruit wines made from naturally sour fruits like gooseberry can have a distinct tartness. Given your preference for beverages gentle on the stomach due to mild acid reflux, you might want to try these higher-acidity wines cautiously.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0012-09-15314:7": 4.391887859156926, "clueweb22-en0012-09-15314:1": 4.330905781340832, "clueweb22-en0012-09-15314:0": 4.222626693176433, "clueweb22-en0031-83-05665:8": 4.071859670088979, "clueweb22-en0004-85-07113:2": 4.007098674633093, "clueweb22-en0033-52-19375:12": 3.9405964949712384, "clueweb22-en0040-67-15497:11": 3.769717025957763, "clueweb22-en0014-34-08039:34": 3.679978904094268}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0021-17-15370:5": 4.524564319455409, "clueweb22-en0040-18-06522:7": 4.395732974019597, "clueweb22-en0012-09-15314:7": 4.391887859156926, "clueweb22-en0012-09-15314:1": 4.330905781340832, "clueweb22-en0038-40-03895:5": 4.242974348999324, "clueweb22-en0012-09-15314:0": 4.222626693176433, "clueweb22-en0021-02-10119:4": 4.111765931592469, "clueweb22-en0026-60-073