{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "2-2_11", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "The Okanagan Valley offers a fantastic wine tasting experience with over 100 to 200 wineries to explore. You have a few options for how to enjoy them:\n\nMany wineries invite visitors for free tours and tastings, though most offer tastings for under 10 CAD, which is often waived with a purchase. You can explore independently, picking a region like West Kelowna or East Kelowna, and driving yourself (with a designated driver, of course!).\n\nAlternatively, organized wine tours are very popular and highly recommended. These services often have pre-selected winery stops or can drive you to your preferred locations. Tours can range from 3 to 6 hours and often include transport, tastings, water, and snacks. For example, a top-rated West Kelowna wine tour is $199 CAD and even encourages guests to dress up in costumes!\n\nBeyond standard tastings, many wineries offer unique experiences. Nk'Mip Cellars, North America's first Indigenous-owned winery, offers tours and tastings with food pairings. Mission Hill Family Estate provides barrel room tastings and a beautiful terrace restaurant. You might even find \"mom-and-pop\" places like House of Rose Winery where you can try grape stomping, or Sandhill Wines which offers a 'Blind Tasting Experience'. Tantalus Vineyards features a gallery-style tasting room, and Grey Monk Estate Winery offers tastings with panoramic lake views.\n\nThe region is particularly vibrant during the annual Okanagan Fall Wine Festival, when the valley is in full swing with special wine dinners and celebrations.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0004-57-15420:4": 4.957073500809372, "clueweb22-en0026-89-01654:5": 4.947836648331002, "clueweb22-en0019-67-07756:1": 4.691994604972342, "clueweb22-en0004-57-15420:5": 4.595107011727465, "clueweb22-en0004-57-15420:3": 4.553046418470822, "clueweb22-en0020-37-07638:4": 4.38098