{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_1", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "As a regular climber, finger pain is a common issue, often stemming from overuse injuries to your fingers and flexor tendons. A frequent cause in climbers is damage to the flexor-tendon pulleys, especially the A2 pulley, which can result from poor warm-ups, dynamic moves, or excessive full crimping.\n\nFor initial self-care, it's crucial to listen to your body. If you experience acute pain, take a break from climbing, or opt for less finger-intensive routes. Avoid pushing through the pain, as recovery can take months. Try to prevent full crimping and use open-hand or half-crimp grips when possible.\n\nAt home, you can apply ice packs to the affected finger for about 20 minutes a few times a day. Rest and reduce any repetitive motions that might be contributing to the pain. Regular stretching of your finger muscles and a good diet can help reduce inflammation. Always warm up your fingers thoroughly before climbing and consider a cool-down routine afterward, including hand massages. If the pain persists or worsens, or if you suspect a serious injury, it's advisable to consult a sports medicine doctor or physiotherapist.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0021-66-06454:13": 4.8170859518466145, "clueweb22-en0041-52-01391:1": 4.488363179733703, "clueweb22-en0023-44-07422:2": 4.486314584958039, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": 4.367041986889895, "clueweb22-en0021-66-06454:12": 4.362667400688729, "clueweb22-en0023-44-07422:3": 4.2899734273371495, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:1": 4.214754312118293, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:3": 4.199963331151667, "clueweb22-en0041-52-01391:2": 4.179225235968068, "clueweb22-en0041-52-01391:3": 4.122288490497047}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0021-66-06454:13": 4.8170859518466145, "clueweb22-en0008-84-09982:22": 4.629684188943141, "clueweb22-en0041-52-01391:1": 4.488363179733703, "clu