{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_2", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Finger pain is a common concern for climbers, often stemming from the significant stress placed on the hands and fingers during the activity. Overuse injuries of the flexor tendons are particularly common, and recovery can sometimes take months. The crimp-grip position, frequently used in climbing, can place considerable strain on the tendon sheaths, potentially leading to injuries like strains or even ruptures. Other potential issues include strains to ligaments, capsules, or muscles in the fingers.\n\nIf you're experiencing finger pain that you suspect is climbing-related, it's crucial to listen to your body. General advice includes taking a break from climbing, especially if the pain is acute, or opting for less finger-intensive routes. It's often recommended to avoid full crimping and instead use open-hand or half-crimp grips when possible. Always warm up your fingers before climbing.\n\nGiven that finger pain in rock climbers is a recognized medical issue, if your pain is severe, persistent, or affects your ability to bend your finger, it's highly advisable to consult a doctor. Early evaluation is important, especially for suspected tendon injuries.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0008-84-09982:22": 4.937975618144465, "clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:13": 4.621677635256268, "clueweb22-en0021-66-06454:13": 4.58867553737263, "clueweb22-en0021-66-06454:12": 4.2245848710880205, "clueweb22-en0014-41-15217:6": 4.074661047031951, "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:17": 3.9119809613284793}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0008-84-09982:22": 4.937975618144465, "clueweb22-en0001-34-18428:18": 4.778526368074747, "clueweb22-en0038-33-02567:13": 4.777246796041271, "clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:13": 4.621677635256268, "clueweb22-en0021-66-06454:13": 4.58867553737263, "clueweb22-en0016-25-19413:13": 4.548185446745279, "clueweb22-en