{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_3", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Your fingers are complex structures designed for movement and grip. Each finger (except the thumb) has three bones called phalanges: the proximal (closest to the palm), middle, and distal (farthest from the palm). The thumb has two phalanges. These bones are connected by hinged joints, including the metacarpophalangeal (MCP) joint at the knuckle, and interphalangeal joints (PIP and DIP) along the finger.\n\nThere are no muscles within the fingers themselves. Instead, movement is controlled by muscles located in your forearm and hand. These muscles connect to the finger bones via strong, cord-like tissues called tendons. Tendons on the top of your hand are called extensor tendons, which straighten your fingers. Tendons on the palm side are flexor tendons, responsible for bending your fingers.\n\nTo ensure smooth movement, these tendons slide through snug tunnels called tendon sheaths, which keep them close to the bones and provide lubrication. Strong bands of tissue called ligaments connect bone to bone, providing stability to the joints. In climbers, an area of particular concern for injury are the annular pulleys, which are thickened areas of connective tissue encasing the flexor tendons, keeping them close to the bone when bending the fingers.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0040-99-11570:2": 3.10325551083485, "clueweb22-en0034-76-16227:1": 3.619651897812545, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:1": 3.5272391143579793, "clueweb22-en0014-41-15217:1": 3.483591222982982, "clueweb22-en0000-37-11836:4": 3.4305535116238595, "clueweb22-en0041-27-16844:3": 3.245553532186346, "clueweb22-en0043-12-04302:2": 3.0201162538252904}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0008-49-08589:5": 4.1380861426168405, "clueweb22-en0008-49-08589:4": 3.9280814470875463, "clueweb22-en0034-76-16227:1": 3.619651897812545, "clueweb22-en0013-70-02989: