{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_4", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Understanding the location and type of pain can help identify the injury.\n\nFor **pulley injuries**, especially common A2 pulley injuries, you might experience a loud \"pop\" followed by significant swelling and pain, typically at the base of the finger. This pain is noticeable when trying to straighten or bend the finger. For more severe ruptures (A2-A4), you might observe \"bowstringing,\" which is a bulge at the base of the finger. Pulley pain is often felt on the front of the finger and can be sharp or dull, intensifying when squeezing or using the injured digit.\n\nIf it's a **tendon injury**, you might have difficulty bending one or more finger joints, pain when attempting to bend your finger, tenderness along the palm side of your hand, and sometimes numbness in the fingertip. Pain from tendon injuries can also originate in the wrist and extend into the finger.\n\nFor **ligament and capsular strains** (joint injuries), the pain usually manifests on the side of the joint rather than the front of the finger. For specific joint issues like epiphyseal stress fractures, pain is often located at the dorsal (back) side of the PIP joint with point tenderness.\n\nIn general, for strains or minor tears of either pulleys or tendons, the pain can be sharp or a dull ache.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": 4.981309295195665, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:1": 4.815914679174998, "clueweb22-en0014-41-15217:6": 4.6159063438375165, "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:16": 4.0520537238045975, "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:17": 4.04830244114325, "clueweb22-en0012-60-11476:6": 3.729256513571551, "clueweb22-en0005-83-07260:9": 3.6205011382200913, "clueweb22-en0012-45-12823:1": 3.510599979368024, "clueweb22-en0012-60-11476:10": 3.2971722976696958}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": 4.981309295195665, "cl