{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_6", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Given your symptoms of pain in the front of the finger, especially when crimping, without visible bowstringing, the first crucial step is to seek a diagnosis from a climbing-knowledgeable physician or orthopedic surgeon. They can assess the damage with an ultrasound or MRI to determine the exact grade of your injury.\n\nIf it's a Grade 1 pulley sprain, which aligns with your description, immobilization is typically not needed. Instead, you should begin gentle range-of-motion exercises: bend your finger until it feels tight, hold for 2-3 seconds, relax, and repeat 10 times for several sessions daily. Also, perform the same exercise by straightening the finger. It's important to avoid pushing through any pain during these exercises.\n\nOnce your finger has a full, pain-free range of motion (often one to two weeks), you can gradually begin gentle re-strengthening. If you plan to continue climbing during recovery, H-taping can provide substantial support to the injured pulley, helping to keep the tendons close to the bone and preventing worsening ruptures.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:7": 4.118641313531763, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:6": 3.0519298019995844, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:11": 3.089015451499475, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:6": 3.1837513221518536, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:8": 2.9794875785283783}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": 4.6958782085483195, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:8": 4.215427850594941, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:7": 4.118641313531763, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:1": 4.042325024229237, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:3": 4.0407496257972495, "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:15": 3.8769333195988973, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:12": 3.7597942649288623, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:4": 3.579013913573839, "clueweb22-en0042-73-03077:16": 3.471755187636395, "clueweb22-en0015