{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_8", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "When you return to climbing after your finger injury, it's crucial to take a very gradual approach to prevent re-injury. Start with easy vertical routes, focusing on big holds and good footholds, and keep your sessions short. Avoid overhangs, campus boards, and especially crimping or using tweaky crimps and pockets for several months.\n\nIt's advisable to use prophylactic taping on your finger when you start climbing again. Only progress to harder climbing if easy routes cause no pain. You can begin gentle re-strengthening once you have a full, pain-free range of motion. This might include using a hangboard with controlled movements, avoiding crimping for at least six weeks, or performing exercises like squeezing putty, finger flexions, or rice bucket exercises to gradually build resistance. Massaging your flexor tendons and pulleys can also help prevent scar tissue and improve flexibility. Continue with stretching and strength-training exercises long-term as preventive measures.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:5": 4.850689302670952, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:11": 4.805872904473031, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:12": 4.5872511869525825, "clueweb22-en0030-70-18403:7": 4.414916358184322, "clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:6": 4.205288960605195, "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:14": 4.150677500909799, "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:13": 4.097434334128242, "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:12": 3.9590512966558804}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:5": 4.850689302670952, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:11": 4.805872904473031, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:12": 4.5872511869525825, "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:0": 4.524688944090263, "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:1": 4.47901982643739, "clueweb22-en0005-83-07260:3": 4.452148129471505, "clueweb22-en0030-70-18403:7": 4.414916358184322, "clueweb22-en0005-83-07260:2": 4.3886378877