{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_9", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "When returning to climbing after an A2 pulley injury, H-taping is recommended as it provides substantial support to your pulley and helps keep the tendons close to the bone, especially when crimping. Research suggests it can even increase the crimping strength of injured fingers.\n\nHere's how to apply H-taping:\n1.  Cut a piece of rigid athletic tape (like Leukoplast brand) that is 10cm long and 1.5cm wide.\n2.  Cut the tape in half lengthwise from both ends, leaving a 1cm bridge in the middle.\n3.  Place the tape on your injured finger so the 1cm bridge sits on the inside of the finger, specifically on the crease of the joint at the upper end of the injured pulley.\n4.  Flex your finger 60\u00b0 at the joint you are taping.\n5.  Wrap the upper part of the tape around the finger to form a ring, then wrap the other part of the tape below the joint to form a second ring.\n\nIt's crucial to retape your finger between each climb or whenever you feel the tape slackening, as tape stretches quickly and its support will diminish. Taping also serves as a helpful psychological reminder of your previous injury.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:6": 4.949865615458739, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:5": 4.720306202573199, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:11": 4.683738646411669, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:8": 4.662146269168885, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:10": 4.506346216151355, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:9": 4.321352603259315, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:13": 4.078007687223599, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:14": 3.599983102981871, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:8": 3.5619144325288667, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:7": 3.558884560967053}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:6": 4.949865615458739, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:12": 4.940801728945573, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:7": 4.8118626329636, "clueweb22-en0033-87-097