{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-2_9", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Practicing yoga at home is an excellent and often more affordable alternative to studio classes or retreats. You have several options to get started without significant expense.\n\nThe most budget-friendly solution is to utilize free resources like YouTube tutorials. There are thousands of channels offering a wide variety of yoga videos, allowing you to browse and find instructors and styles that suit you, though quality can vary.\n\nAnother option is to subscribe to online yoga services or apps, such as Glo or Gaia. While these typically involve a monthly fee, it's usually much lower than studio memberships, and they often provide access to a vast library of high-quality, vetted instruction tailored to different needs. Many also offer free trials.\n\nFor equipment, the initial costs are minimal. You'll primarily need comfortable clothing and a yoga mat; most studios provide mats, or you can purchase one for a one-time cost. Optional props like blocks, straps, or bolsters can be added later if desired.\n\nTo set up your home practice, find a quiet, comfortable space with enough room to move freely. This could be a dedicated area or even a small corner. You can use a computer, TV, or music speakers for guided videos or soothing music. Practicing at home also offers the benefits of privacy and full control over your sessions.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0008-30-04400:21": 4.890749858943647, "clueweb22-en0003-42-04886:12": 4.546499623067609, "clueweb22-en0009-76-09135:3": 4.530927204848866, "clueweb22-en0009-76-09135:2": 4.307363299215292, "clueweb22-en0038-69-16036:6": 4.157848609511511, "clueweb22-en0005-78-03702:6": 4.1486540138020835}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0008-30-04400:21": 4.890749858943647, "clueweb22-en0035-13-17314:6": 4.635403586752242, "clueweb22-en0003-42-04886:12": 4.5464996230676