{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "4-1_2", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Preparing for a 10-mile run is a great goal, especially since you've mentioned not being very confident in your fitness level. For a beginner, it's highly recommended to choose a training plan designed to gradually ease your body into the distance and help prevent injuries. Many plans suggest you should be able to comfortably run about 3 miles before starting a 10-mile plan, or you could even start with a 15K (9.3 miles) plan, which is very similar and can be used interchangeably.\n\nAn effective strategy is to train for a 10K first, and then spend a few more weeks developing extra endurance for the 10-miler, ideally running the 10K about four weeks before your main race. Your training plan should include regular long runs, progressively increasing in distance (e.g., adding half a mile every other weekend), and cross-training activities like yoga, strength training, or biking to strengthen muscles and prevent injuries. Don't forget to include rest days. When running, aim for a \"conversational\" pace, which is typically one to two minutes per mile slower than your short-run pace.\n\nFor runs over 60 minutes, remember to fuel your body with snacks and stay hydrated with a portable water bottle. A foam roller can also be beneficial for muscle recovery. Planning your nutrition and having the right gear are crucial for a successful training journey.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0019-07-06552:3": 4.799991022611203, "clueweb22-en0025-82-01264:1": 4.773048250783129, "clueweb22-en0004-89-17215:3": 4.647504265460972, "clueweb22-en0008-64-18271:11": 4.6128934648721005, "clueweb22-en0004-89-17215:2": 4.521870999286933, "clueweb22-en0033-80-17520:2": 4.487709946043363, "clueweb22-en0008-64-18271:5": 4.351214074788573, "clueweb22-en0008-64-18271:6": 4.275584743342246, "clueweb22-en0008-64-18271:18": 4.243047023813382, "clueweb22-en0018