{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "4-1_6", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Having 4 months (approximately 16 weeks) to prepare for your 10km run is an excellent amount of time, especially given that you're not very confident in your fitness level. This extended period allows for a gradual and safe build-up, which is crucial for preventing injuries, building endurance, and boosting your confidence.\n\nFor someone starting out or feeling less confident, a 12 to 16-week training plan is ideal. For example, a \"Couch to 10K\" program often spans 13 weeks and is designed for complete beginners with no prior running experience, helping you get race-ready within 3-4 months (Passage 1). Similarly, a 12-week beginner 10K plan is suitable if you can currently run or jog for up to 20 minutes (Passage 15).\n\nThese longer plans typically emphasize a slow and steady increase in mileage and intensity. They often include active recovery weeks, usually every fourth week, where training volume is reduced to allow your body to recover and adapt, minimizing injury risk (Passage 3, 15). Following a structured training plan significantly increases your likelihood of achieving your goal (Passage 3, 15). A 16-week schedule can even allow you to gradually progress from a 5K base, providing a solid foundation for your 10K (Passage 8).", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0042-16-12588:0": 4.678149387313163, "clueweb22-en0004-91-11430:5": 4.393189130877404, "clueweb22-en0000-55-01473:1": 4.079150407829591, "clueweb22-en0004-91-11430:10": 3.9817398313946395}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0042-16-12588:0": 4.678149387313163, "clueweb22-en0025-76-14567:0": 4.406658622529766, "clueweb22-en0004-91-11430:5": 4.393189130877404, "clueweb22-en0004-91-11430:17": 4.323802265088112, "clueweb22-en0002-57-10562:1": 4.321784425808429, "clueweb22-en0002-57-10562:9": 4.289218603503215, "clueweb22-en0004-91-11430:18": 4.085