{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "5-1_10", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "White sugar is highly processed, calorie-dense, and linked to various health issues like obesity, diabetes, inflammation, and heart disease. In contrast, several alternatives offer healthier profiles.\n\n**Stevia** is widely considered the healthiest option. It's calorie-free, doesn't raise blood sugar, and is beneficial for diabetics, even improving insulin sensitivity. However, some users report side effects like bloating, nausea, dizziness, or muscle pain. There's ongoing research regarding its long-term effects on blood pressure and potential genetic mutations, though some studies suggest it may help prevent kidney damage. The FDA has approved only purified stevia (stevioside) as safe.\n\n**Xylitol, Erythritol, and Yacon Syrup** are also good alternatives. Xylitol has nearly 40% fewer calories than sugar, doesn't spike blood sugar or insulin, contains zero fructose, and can prevent tooth decay, though too much may cause digestive issues and it's highly toxic to dogs. Erythritol is very low in calories (6% of sugar's), doesn't affect blood sugar, cholesterol, or insulin, and is generally well-tolerated, though it's more expensive. Yacon syrup, from a South American plant, aids digestion with antioxidants and fiber and may be linked to weight loss. These sugar alcohols generally have lower glycemic indexes and don't spike blood sugar like regular sugar.\n\nNatural sweeteners like **honey, molasses, and maple syrup** are less harmful than white sugar and offer some vitamins, minerals, and antioxidants. Honey has a lower glycemic index than sugar, meaning it raises blood sugar more slowly, but has slightly more calories per teaspoon. Molasses is rich in antioxidants and good for bone/heart health, but is calorie-dense and not ideal for diabetics. Maple syrup has similar carbs and calories to white sugar per tablespoon. While these natural options have benefits, they are still calorie-rich and should be used sparingly, as moderation is key for all sweeteners.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0045-54-01249:7": 4.989551252965636, "clueweb22-en0009-63-03406:11": 4.3076124134042955, "clueweb22-en0022-82-01437:10": 4.2280319743106265, "clueweb22-en0026-66-11327:17": 4.1388079271756535, "clueweb22-en0045-54-01249:6": 4.053533422831337, "clueweb22-en0044-05-16388:9": 3.9571222849680847, "clueweb22-en0029-01-00630:2": 3.8278280246436145, "clueweb22-en0041-29-11312:5": 3.7933626379716925, "clueweb22-en0022-82-01437:8": 3.7843573627245357, "clueweb22-en0012-96-12663:14": 3.730457421176526, "clueweb22-en0022-70-06923:9": 3.7170465606576433, "clueweb22-en0041-29-11312:6": 3.7008830861469226, "clueweb22-en0016-44-19991:3": 3.7001499727609994, "clueweb22-en0019-54-09248:12": 3.6747997103194416, "clueweb22-en0045-69-05631:13": 3.6673639605988932, "clueweb22-en0046-56-14673:10": 3.6588379507074884, "clueweb22-en0007-95-06718:1": 3.6552899795901226, "clueweb22-en0046-56-14673:9": 3.608809090433761, "clueweb22-en0019-54-09248:13": 3.603942168515154, "clueweb22-en0015-97-13885:15": 3.5578419684783675}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0045-54-01249:7": 4.989551252965636, "clueweb22-en0009-63-03406:11": 4.3076124134042955, "clueweb22-en0022-82-01437:10": 4.2280319743106265, "clueweb22-en0026-66-11327:17": 4.1388079271756535, "clueweb22-en0045-54-01249:6": 4.053533422831337, "clueweb22-en0044-05-16388:9": 3.9571222849680847, "clueweb22-en0029-01-00630:2": 3.8278280246436145, "clueweb22-en0041-29-11312:5": 3.7933626379716925, "clueweb22-en0022-82-01437:8": 3.7843573627245357, "clueweb22-en0012-96-12663:14": 3.730457421176526, "clueweb22-en0022-70-06923:9": 3.7170465606576433, "clueweb22-en0041-29-11312:6": 3.7008830861469226, "clueweb22-en0016-44-19991:3": 3.7001499727609994, "clueweb22-en0019-54-09248:12": 3.6747997103194416, "clueweb22-en0045-69-05631:13": 3.6673639605988932, "clueweb22-