{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "5-2_4", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Yes, you will need to make some adjustments to your diet during pregnancy, but you don't necessarily need to go on a special diet or \"eat for two.\" The main focus should be on eating a healthy, varied, and nutrient-dense diet to support both your health and your baby's development.\n\nWhile your basic healthy eating principles remain, your nutritional needs will be greater. You'll typically need a small increase in calories, especially in your second and third trimesters (around 300-500 extra calories per day). It's important to get enough proteins, carbohydrates, healthy fats, and essential minerals and vitamins from a variety of food groups, including fruits, vegetables, whole grains, lean meats, and dairy products.\n\nYou should also be aware of certain foods to limit or avoid entirely. These include high-mercury fish (like shark, swordfish, marlin), certain soft cheeses (like brie and camembert), raw or undercooked meats, eggs, and seafood, as well as liver and p\u00e2t\u00e9. Alcohol should also be eliminated. Additionally, ensure you are getting enough folic acid, iron, and calcium, as these are particularly important during pregnancy. It's best to get most vitamins from food, but a folic acid supplement is generally recommended.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0012-37-11383:13": 4.869335264523176, "clueweb22-en0000-09-19416:0": 4.779112298268847, "clueweb22-en0013-81-18321:1": 4.587111394883357, "clueweb22-en0027-89-15500:0": 4.5770829432875475, "clueweb22-en0022-69-17702:17": 4.493075418921404, "clueweb22-en0028-81-12071:2": 4.3915130504006346, "clueweb22-en0028-37-01996:1": 4.347616556207245, "clueweb22-en0001-10-19534:7": 4.3315872142714325, "clueweb22-en0026-42-08361:1": 4.305360518659262, "clueweb22-en0008-09-13508:1": 4.303242011773741, "clueweb22-en0035-01-11070:0": 4.285720404110853, "clueweb22-en0008-88-0041