{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "5-2_5", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "During pregnancy, you don't typically need to go on a special or drastically different diet. Instead, the focus should be on maintaining a healthy, balanced, and varied diet to ensure you and your baby get all the necessary nutrients.\n\nThe idea of 'eating for two' is a myth; your energy requirements don't change much in the first trimester, but you'll need a little extra energy\u2014around 200-500 calories more per day\u2014in the second and third trimesters.\n\nPrioritize nutrient-dense, whole foods from all major food groups: plenty of fruits and vegetables, whole grains, lean proteins (like meat, fish, eggs, beans), and moderate amounts of dairy products. You should limit foods high in fat and sugar.\n\nIt's crucial to avoid certain foods that could be harmful, such as high-mercury fish (like shark, swordfish, marlin), some soft cheeses (like brie, camembert) that may contain listeria, raw or undercooked meats, eggs, and poultry. Alcohol should also be eliminated. Additionally, a folic acid supplement is generally recommended.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0012-37-11383:13": 4.869335264523176, "clueweb22-en0000-09-19416:0": 4.779112298268847, "clueweb22-en0027-89-15500:0": 4.5770829432875475, "clueweb22-en0022-69-17702:17": 4.493075418921404, "clueweb22-en0028-81-12071:2": 4.3915130504006346, "clueweb22-en0028-37-01996:1": 4.347616556207245, "clueweb22-en0001-10-19534:7": 4.3315872142714325, "clueweb22-en0026-42-08361:1": 4.305360518659262, "clueweb22-en0008-09-13508:1": 4.303242011773741, "clueweb22-en0028-37-01996:2": 4.286937195264285, "clueweb22-en0035-01-11070:0": 4.285720404110853, "clueweb22-en0008-88-00414:0": 4.261513393557893}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0012-37-11383:13": 4.869335264523176, "clueweb22-en0000-09-19416:0": 4.779112298268847, "clueweb22-en0014-61-10226:3": 4.6082915857