{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "5-2_7", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "While breastfeeding, you generally don't need to follow a special or restrictive diet, but focusing on a balanced and varied intake is important for your energy and overall health. Many of the healthy eating habits you adopted during pregnancy can continue.\n\nAim to eat a wide variety of nutrient-dense foods:\n*   **Fruits and Vegetables:** Eat plenty of colorful fruits and vegetables.\n*   **Whole Grains:** Choose whole-wheat bread, brown rice, and whole-wheat pasta more often for added nutrients and fiber. Your preferred white rice and cake can still be part of a balanced diet as sources of carbohydrates, which are important for energy.\n*   **Protein:** Include lean meats, poultry, eggs, fish (especially fatty fish like salmon), beans, lentils, nuts, and seeds.\n*   **Dairy/Calcium:** Ensure you get enough calcium from milk, yogurt, cheese, or fortified alternatives like soy milk. Your body prioritizes your baby's needs, so adequate calcium intake helps protect your own bone health.\n*   **Healthy Fats:** Incorporate healthy oils like olive oil and fats from foods like avocado and fish.\n\nYou'll likely feel hungrier and thirstier, needing about 500 extra calories per day. Focus on drinking plenty of water and other unsweetened, decaffeinated beverages.\n\nIt's recommended to limit caffeine (to about 200-300mg daily) as it can pass into your milk and affect your baby's sleep. Also, be mindful of alcohol consumption, allowing 2-3 hours per drink before breastfeeding. Avoid excessive sugary drinks and \"empty calories.\"\n\nContinuing your prenatal multivitamin may also be recommended. If you have specific dietary concerns or needs, consulting a registered dietitian or your doctor is always a good idea.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0034-54-11087:7": 5.0, "clueweb22-en0029-93-12021:2": 4.641905675521469, "clueweb22-en0038