{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "5-2_8", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Yes, consuming alcohol while breastfeeding can increase the risk of your baby not getting enough milk. Studies show that alcohol can reduce your milk supply by affecting the hormones that control milk production, such as oxytocin, and by hindering the milk ejection reflex (let-down). Even small amounts of alcohol can disrupt this balance.\n\nAdditionally, alcohol in breast milk can change its taste and smell, which may lead babies to feed less or refuse to feed. Research indicates that babies may consume significantly less milk in the hours following their mother's alcohol consumption, with some studies showing a 20-23% decrease. While occasional, moderate drinking is unlikely to cause long-term issues, regular or heavy alcohol use has the potential to chronically lower your milk supply and can compromise your baby's growth and development due to decreased milk intake. Therefore, health experts generally advise avoiding alcohol or consuming it cautiously and in moderation, timing feeds to minimize exposure.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0046-33-11913:13": 4.641749026901534, "clueweb22-en0034-22-10973:1": 4.548016642143065, "clueweb22-en0046-33-11913:10": 4.492998499542685, "clueweb22-en0046-33-11913:9": 4.463147694716155, "clueweb22-en0033-22-04249:4": 4.461506592171526, "clueweb22-en0004-28-10513:4": 4.460544313207544, "clueweb22-en0046-33-11913:11": 4.4513527909607395, "clueweb22-en0002-39-11926:5": 4.40032334569553, "clueweb22-en0046-33-11913:12": 4.392715177534374, "clueweb22-en0039-19-13514:0": 4.382630518023708, "clueweb22-en0046-33-11913:8": 4.346687531376029, "clueweb22-en0005-68-03159:4": 4.340219485603538}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0031-57-16983:0": 4.816922454860461, "clueweb22-en0042-99-06708:12": 4.730438985233968, "clueweb22-en0046-33-11913:13": 4.641749026901534, "clueweb22-en0034-