{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "5-2_10", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "It's completely normal for newborn babies to cry, and it's their primary way of communicating their needs. On average, newborns typically cry for about 2 to 3 hours a day, especially during their first few months of life. This crying is usually spaced out throughout the day. You might notice that crying often increases around 2 to 3 weeks of age and peaks by about 6 to 8 weeks, then gradually decreases. By around 3 months, babies often cry for about an hour a day.\n\nBabies cry for various reasons, such as hunger, discomfort, needing a diaper change, tiredness, or simply a need for your attention and comfort. While it can be distressing to hear, remember that by holding and comforting your baby, you are teaching them they are not alone. If your baby cries excessively, for example, for more than 3 hours a day, more than 3 days a week, for 3 weeks or more, it could be colic, which is also a common and temporary phase for many infants.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0038-57-04444:0": 4.913303832551309, "clueweb22-en0029-92-05890:2": 4.768525541550765, "clueweb22-en0043-40-01751:2": 4.621817694880032, "clueweb22-en0022-43-13342:10": 4.5855952737947865, "clueweb22-en0013-59-01598:13": 4.509243857723886, "clueweb22-en0001-28-08060:7": 4.471749715894322, "clueweb22-en0029-92-05890:1": 4.410980052654944, "clueweb22-en0000-45-00661:0": 4.358595485053654, "clueweb22-en0016-36-07010:1": 4.232066386526439, "clueweb22-en0045-47-16801:1": 4.192985688447172, "clueweb22-en0011-17-01403:7": 4.175975215512808, "clueweb22-en0026-56-09659:2": 4.173609610346614, "clueweb22-en0001-87-12954:4": 4.1319714139408115, "clueweb22-en0026-56-09659:1": 4.101769977993683, "clueweb22-en0022-43-13342:9": 4.0913816006483685, "clueweb22-en0015-34-03797:3": 4.09096073634364, "clueweb22-en0043-40-01751:1": 4.067381075043695, "clueweb22-en0013-59-01598:12": 4.03