{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "8-1_6", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "I understand that the rigid structure of the Pomodoro Technique and its interruption of your hyperfocus can be counterproductive, especially when you're in a flow state. Many people find that forcing breaks can disrupt their concentration rather than enhance it.\n\nA great alternative that allows for more flexibility and respects your natural focus periods is the **Flowtime Technique**. Instead of setting a timer, you simply start working on a single task and record your start time. You continue working for as long as you feel focused and productive, without any alarms or forced interruptions. When you naturally feel your focus waning, or you need a break, you stop and record your end time. The length of your break is also flexible, based on how long you worked. This method allows you to ride your waves of hyperfocus without being pulled out prematurely.\n\nIf you still prefer some form of timed structure but need more flexibility, you could also try **customizing the Pomodoro intervals**. Instead of 25 minutes, you might experiment with longer work periods, like 50 or 90 minutes, to better match your natural attention span and allow for deeper focus before taking a break.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0022-63-14535:8": 4.737435020721773, "clueweb22-en0000-09-08980:8": 4.69534598049508, "clueweb22-en0038-55-03763:3": 4.407683641778137, "clueweb22-en0015-94-05578:2": 3.9475976660651657, "clueweb22-en0006-66-05593:7": 3.9152920707688015, "clueweb22-en0008-67-15474:14": 3.745945894503908, "clueweb22-en0038-55-03763:4": 3.699488355986846, "clueweb22-en0038-55-03763:5": 3.690136082606102, "clueweb22-en0010-71-10871:15": 3.6574097142061657, "clueweb22-en0015-94-05578:0": 3.657370278929399}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0022-63-14535:8": 4.737435020721773, "clueweb22-en0000-09-08980:8": 4.69534598049508, "c