{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "8-1_7", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Absolutely, maintaining hyperfocus while managing external interruptions like emails and meetings is a common challenge, but there are effective strategies to help.\n\nFor **emails**, a key approach is to avoid checking them constantly. Instead, set specific times during the day to check and respond to non-urgent emails, perhaps first thing in the morning, before lunch, and at the end of the day. You can turn off audible and visual notifications to prevent them from pulling you out of your flow. Consider setting up an autoresponder that directs urgent requests to a phone call or in-person contact. Jotting down any distracting thoughts or ideas that pop up can also help you stay on task, knowing you'll address them later.\n\nFor **scheduled meetings**, electronic reminders can be very helpful to alert you a few minutes beforehand, allowing you to transition out of hyperfocus. Some individuals find it effective to compartmentalize their workweek, dedicating specific days or blocks of time solely to meetings, and reserving other days for deep, uninterrupted work. You can also proactively manage meetings by questioning recurring ones or challenging the attendance list to ensure they are truly necessary and efficient.\n\nFinally, to manage the transition out of hyperfocus, you could set a gentle timer (e.g., 15 minutes) before an anticipated interruption. This gives you a soft landing rather than an abrupt stop. Some people also find it helpful to communicate their focused work periods to colleagues, or even ask a trusted co-worker to gently interrupt them when necessary.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0025-75-16053:1": 4.902090135410124, "clueweb22-en0038-88-03812:8": 4.655872495263261, "clueweb22-en0038-88-03812:7": 4.422515589048394, "clueweb22-en0025-75-16053:2": 4.399085617202572, "clueweb22-en0030-54-15087:16": 4.3850571591