{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "8-1_8", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "To prevent scheduled meetings from disrupting your work momentum and hyperfocus, preparation and strategic scheduling are key.\n\nFirst, **schedule dedicated prep and follow-up time** around your meetings. This helps contain their impact on your day, allowing you to review materials, define your contribution, and complete any immediate follow-up tasks without breaking your flow elsewhere (Passages 2, 9, 12, 17).\n\nSecond, ensure meetings have a **clear purpose and a timed agenda** shared in advance. This keeps discussions focused and productive, reducing wasted time and ensuring everyone comes prepared (Passages 3, 8, 19). If you're organizing, include pre-work for participants (Passages 10, 11).\n\nThird, **strategically manage your calendar**:\n*   **Schedule meetings back-to-back** to minimize context switching (Passage 1).\n*   **Block out \"focus days\" or large chunks of time** as \"busy\" to protect your deep work periods from interruptions (Passages 2, 6).\n*   **Push back on unnecessary invitations** or suggest alternative communication methods like email updates if your physical presence isn't essential (Passage 4, 6).\n*   If possible, plan meetings during natural productivity slumps rather than your peak focus times (Passage 16).\n\nBy proactively preparing and managing your meeting schedule, you can minimize their disruptive effect on your concentration.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0009-95-04988:7": 4.860726200665291, "clueweb22-en0016-28-01007:8": 4.8063264935514685, "clueweb22-en0007-09-16100:3": 4.767115911846665, "clueweb22-en0010-30-18704:4": 4.707854944560017, "clueweb22-en0020-51-10719:15": 4.537239563098222, "clueweb22-en0044-68-04588:6": 4.475236711969617, "clueweb22-en0007-09-16100:1": 4.450210223671523, "clueweb22-en0016-28-01007:9": 4.439340698036808, "clueweb22-en0023-66-19436:3": 4.395024684828