{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "8-1_9", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Dealing with scheduled meetings that interrupt your hyperfocus can be really disruptive. The key is often to proactively manage your schedule and the meetings themselves, rather than just reacting to them.\n\nOne effective strategy is to **dedicate specific blocks of time or even entire days for meetings**, leaving other times for uninterrupted deep work. For example, you could try scheduling all your meetings on Tuesdays, keeping other days free for hyperfocus. This compartmentalizes the interruptions, allowing for longer stretches of focused work.\n\nYou can also **proactively manage meeting invitations**. Don't be afraid to gently push back on unnecessary meetings, suggest alternative times that are less disruptive to your flow, or offer to provide information via email instead of attending. Encourage shorter, more focused meetings by asking for agendas and only attending if your presence is truly essential. Some teams find success with very short, routine check-ins (e.g., 10-minute daily huddles) to consolidate discussions and reduce ad-hoc interruptions.\n\nFinally, when a meeting is unavoidable and you're in hyperfocus, **build in a short transition time** (e.g., 10-15 minutes) before the meeting starts. This allows your brain to shift gears more smoothly rather than being abruptly pulled away. You could also use a gentle reminder, like a timer, a few minutes before the meeting to help you transition out of your deep work.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0034-10-11301:12": 4.862331752723326, "clueweb22-en0010-30-18704:4": 4.736207774606852, "clueweb22-en0041-12-03522:7": 4.723594897341027, "clueweb22-en0006-62-03234:3": 4.706463718105441, "clueweb22-en0046-52-03977:16": 4.605968318029024, "clueweb22-en0014-63-15541:4": 4.596007060825098, "clueweb22-en0013-60-10409:8": 4.35730635848356, "clueweb22-en0026-39-04603:13": 4.